<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyinbya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pyinbya was an early king of Pagan who, according to the Burmese chronicles, reigned in the mid-ninth century and fortified the city in 849 on a bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy River</a>. Little else is known of him, and modern historians treat the early chronicle dates with caution, but the date of 849 has long been taken as the traditional founding of Pagan as a walled city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyinbya.jpg" alt="Pyinbya" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tharaba_Gate.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the Burmese chronicles, Pyinbya was a son of King Saw Khin Hnit of Pagan, born in 817 or, by another reckoning, 802. Before his accession he was lord of Pyinbya, a village of Pagan, and was known as Pyinbya Mintha. He succeeded his brother, King Khelu, in 846. The chronicles count him as the 33rd king of a dynasty founded in the early 2nd century, but they do not agree on his regnal years or on the years of his birth and death; the oldest of them, the Zatadawbon Yazawin, is considered the most accurate for the Pagan period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The chronicles date the founding of the city of Pagan to 23 December 849 and a settlement at Taungdwin to 857, perhaps the southern limit of his young kingdom. Mainstream scholarship sees Pagan as founded by the Mranma, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a>, of the Nanzhao Kingdom, and as the first known Burman settlement in the Irrawaddy valley. It arose after raids by Nanzhao into the upper valley, from the 750s to the 830s, had left the Pyu states severely weakened and many Mranma warriors and their families in the region. The site lay close to the Chindwin river and just west of a richly irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> plain, and may have been meant to help Nanzhao pacify the countryside.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The chronicles say Pyinbya died in 876, at the age of 59, and was succeeded by his son Tannet. Pagan remained one of several competing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> until the late 10th century, when it grew in authority and grandeur. Two hundred years after Pyinbya founded the city, his great-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> created the Pagan Empire, the first unification of the Irrawaddy valley and its periphery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pyinbya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The newcomers settled first on the irrigated plain of Kyaukse and then along the middle Irrawaddy, and the Burmese chronicles date the fortification of Pagan, on a bend of the river, to 849 under a king named <b>Pyinbya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Burmese chronicles, Bagan was founded in the second century CE and fortified in 849 by King <b>Pyinbya</b>, the 34th successor of the founder of early Bagan.</blockquote><a href="/work/bagan">Bagan · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=864" aria-label="Bagan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Pyinbya"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5387 5744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5387 5744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=864">What was here in 864 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
